{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/pulls/2836","id":4391042713,"node_id":"PR_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAABBbn-mQ","html_url":"https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2836","diff_url":"https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2836.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2836.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/issues/2836","number":2836,"state":"open","locked":false,"title":"Apply SaBRe physical-exit reports during scheduler maintenance","user":{"login":"rrnewton","id":274582,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3NDU4Mg==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/274582?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton","html_url":"https://github.com/rrnewton","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"[hermit2, degraded-unresolved, gpt-6-astra, devbig014, role=impl]\nUnverified --who metadata: dev-hermit\n\n## Plain Language Summary and Project Impact\n\nSaBRe could finish two otherwise matching runs with different INFO shutdown events because the host's physical-exit report raced the scheduler's completion check. The backend now records that report separately, and scheduler maintenance applies it under the same lock used to decide completion. Consuming the logical child wait cannot erase an unprocessed report or make the child waitable twice.\n\nThis change addresses the race between the scheduler's loop-top completion check and its step2 completion check. It does not establish that the broader SaBRe divergence in a guest that forks and reaps with wait4(WNOHANG) is fixed; that investigation remains open. The retained root-process and sched-setattr workloads qualify the completion-report ordering described here. They do not qualify the separate fork/reap workload recorded in the scheduler comment.\n\nThis recovers the remaining scheduler and all-feature diagnostic work from the original 38-path, 19-commit proposal on current main. Its validation graph, typed termination, prepared Nextest and CPU-evidence work is already preserved through https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2970, https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2822, https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2833 and https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2966. The current single DAG, width limits, dependency pin, result schemas, selected cells and measurement history remain intact. The original public source is preserved separately before replacement.\n\nTask: `astra-lane-drains-hermit-reverie-pr-queues-to-zero`.\n\n## Summary\n\n- Keep physical exit reporting separate from scheduler application, including report-before-wait and wait-before-report orderings and exec identity reuse.\n- Retain the three original scheduler tests and add a public-callback/actual-scheduler completion control.\n- Preserve all INFO events and strengthen both retained SaBRe log checks with the original zero-fizzle assertion.\n- Restore the original backend diagnostic assertions under all features. Update only the measured Detcore test count from 667 to 671; all other generated DAG values remain unchanged.\n\n## Determinism\n\nThe asynchronous callback records a fact without choosing a scheduler completion outcome. The scheduler drains reports under its lock immediately before the loop-top completion check. Existing physical-exit barriers, pending admission, waiter/timer logic and child membership remain authoritative. A duplicate begin retains its pending report; a fresh incarnation clears stale completion state.\n\nThe strict BitwiseInfoV1 policy, complete INFO record comparison, I/O buffers, virtual time, exact remainder, selected cells, CPU/wall limits and retry policy are unchanged. No log is hidden or demoted. The separate waiter-liveness proposal at https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2747 is not included.\n\n## Linux Semantics\n\nThe supervisor still observes the final kernel wait status. Guest wait ownership and status consumption remain separate from the backend physical report: consuming a wait cannot erase that report, and applying a report cannot resurrect a consumed child. Current child-TID gating and signal-mask ABI behavior from https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/2985 are preserved. SaBRe is the enabled reporting backend; this is not a claim about unresolved KVM physical-exit or signal work.\n\n## Relationship to gVisor\n\nThis change does not use or modify gVisor.\n\n## Validation\n\nCandidate: `1948e95fe14cc95687e886704420a9922a1fe1cb`, based on main `eec5e18affd17229f86b3943520ee2b9821a377d`.\n\n- Native combined Detcore population: 671 passed, zero failed/ignored/filtered; 15.904 s including build. All 667 main identities and metadata remain, with exactly four additions. Final Clippy with warnings denied, formatting and graph freshness passed. The graph's 1,388 nodes differ only in that exact count.\n- The current-source public callback fails before the repair. Restoring the missing maintenance drain or deleting a pending report causes the named controls to fail; the defective all-feature diagnostic also fails the original assertion. All old failures remain retained.\n- Contained build: all 15 commands passed, with 16 GiB memory, zero swap, eight CPU equivalents, 4,096 tasks and bounded captures. Actual compiled SHA is `1948e95fe14c`; published Hermit SHA256 is `b3765c35a181536b40755dd04ef0d08735a329230e279a37ea8c6a13974b15da`.\n- Official `c-programs/sched-setattr-batch` verify/SaBRe qualification: 20/20 on attempt one, 5.948 s total, maximum width 8 and per-harness width 1. Every run retains CPU 22 s/wall 57 s. All 40 raw logs have exactly one scheduler completion before fallback completion and no fizzle event. Each comparison contains 103 INFO records per side, with full canonical policy, equal I/O and no relaxation. Both executions in every pair record guest RPC activity, zero ptrace fallback sites and zero trusted shared-object sites. Maximum observed cell CPU was 535,493 us and wall was 690 ms.\n- Existing five-test `test.sabre_examples` node: local off-record driver exit zero in 97.818 s; node wall 60.605 s, Nextest execution 9.805 s. The retained typed CPU/result reconciliation records exactly the five expected identities, each attempt one and exit zero. Prepared executable/runtime hashes and the existing node CPU 7,200 s/wall 900 s remain intact. The Nextest build-script-output warnings are retained; no test was skipped.\n\nThe focused run published no receipt or ledger row. These results do not claim full-main validation, repeat the original width-20 campaign, or turn the historical full-profile receipt into current evidence. Complete original source accounting and immutable build/native/guest artifacts are recorded in the root TaskGraph notes.\n\n## Human Review Required\n\nPost-facto human review trigger 4 applies: this changes when core Detcore scheduling incorporates an asynchronously reported physical process exit. Exact candidate and base are stated above. The all-feature diagnostic and measured-count updates do not introduce another trigger.\n","created_at":"2026-08-29T10:07:06Z","updated_at":"2026-09-15T23:35:04Z","closed_at":null,"merged_at":null,"merge_commit_sha":null,"assignees":[],"requested_reviewers":[],"requested_teams":[],"labels":[{"id":11603596200,"node_id":"LA_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAACs6DTqA","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/labels/post-facto-human-review","name":"post-facto-human-review","color":"FFA500","default":false,"description":"Landed under post-facto protocol; needs human review","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null},{"id":11681812218,"node_id":"LA_kwDOIZiOrM8AAAACuEpO-g","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/labels/adversarial-review-claude1","name":"adversarial-review-claude1","color":"1D76DB","default":false,"description":"Adversarial review pass, claude lane (round 1)","archived_at":null,"archived_by":null}],"milestone":null,"draft":false,"commits_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/pulls/2836/commits","review_comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/pulls/2836/comments","review_comment_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/pulls/comments{/number}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/issues/2836/comments","statuses_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/statuses/1948e95fe14cc95687e886704420a9922a1fe1cb","head":{"label":"rrnewton:degraded-ci-hub/sabre-shutdown-order","ref":"degraded-ci-hub/sabre-shutdown-order","sha":"1948e95fe14cc95687e886704420a9922a1fe1cb","user":{"login":"rrnewton","id":274582,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3NDU4Mg==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/274582?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton","html_url":"https://github.com/rrnewton","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"repo":{"id":563646124,"node_id":"R_kgDOIZiOrA","name":"hermit","full_name":"rrnewton/hermit","private":false,"owner":{"login":"rrnewton","id":274582,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI3NDU4Mg==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/274582?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton","html_url":"https://github.com/rrnewton","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rrnewton/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"html_url":"https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit","description":"Hermit launches linux x86_64 programs in a special, hermetically isolated sandbox to control their execution. Hermit translates normal, nondeterministic behavior, into deterministic, repeatable behavior. This can be used for various applications, including replay-debugging, reproducible artifacts, chaos mode concurrency testing and bug analysis.","fork":true,"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit","forks_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/forks","keys_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/keys{/key_id}","collaborators_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/collaborators{/collaborator}","teams_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/teams","hooks_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/rrnewton/hermit/hooks","issue_events_ur